1 . Amstrong Number :

import java.util.Scanner;

public class ArmstrongNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int num = sc.nextInt();

        // Copy for checking later
        int original = num;
        int result = 0;

        // Count digits
        int n = String.valueOf(num).length();

        // Calculate sum of powers of digits
        while (num > 0) {
            int digit = num % 10;         // get last digit
            result += Math.pow(digit, n); // add digit^n
            num /= 10;                    // remove last digit
        }

        // Check Armstrong condition
        if (result == original) {
            System.out.println(original + " is an Armstrong number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(original + " is NOT an Armstrong number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
Enter a number: 153
153 is an Armstrong number.

import java.util.*;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int original = n;
        int ams = 0;
        int N = String.valueOf(n).length();
        
        while(n > 0) {
            int ld = n % 10;
            ams = ams + (int)Math.pow(ld, N);  // fixed
            n /= 10;
        }
        
        if(original == ams) {
            System.out.print("Armstrong Number");
        } else {
            System.out.print("Not an Armstrong");
        }
    }
}

Math.pow(ld, N) always returns a double, even if the inputs are integers.

But in your code, ams is declared as an int, so Java complains about “possible lossy conversion from double to int”.


2.Palindrome :
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PalindromeNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int num = sc.nextInt();

        int original = num;
        int reversed = 0;

        // Reverse the number
        while (num > 0) {
            int digit = num % 10;          // extract last digit
            reversed = reversed * 10 + digit; // build reversed number
            num /= 10;                     // remove last digit
        }

        // Check palindrome condition
        if (original == reversed) {
            System.out.println(original + " is a Palindrome number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(original + " is NOT a Palindrome number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

