4. Prime Number

Optimal Approach :
A prime number is a number greater than 1 that has only two divisors: 1 and itself.

Examples:

Prime → 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13

Not Prime → 4 (divisible by 2), 9 (divisible by 3)
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        boolean isPrime = true;

        // Prime check: number must be > 1
        if (n <= 1) {
            isPrime = false;
        } else {
            // Check divisibility from 2 to sqrt(n)
            for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
                if (n % i == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Output result
        if (isPrime) {
            System.out.println(n + " is a Prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(n + " is NOT a Prime number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

Brute Force Approach :
import java.util.Scanner;

public class PrimeBruteForce {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input number
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        boolean isPrime = true;

        // Prime must be greater than 1
        if (n <= 1) {
            isPrime = false;
        } else {
            // Check divisibility from 2 to n-1
            for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
                if (n % i == 0) {
                    isPrime = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Output result
        if (isPrime) {
            System.out.println(n + " is a Prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(n + " is NOT a Prime number.");
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

Time Complexity : O(root n)
1. O(log n)

Means the number of steps grows very slowly as n increases.

Usually comes from problems where input is divided in half each step.

Example: Binary Search

For n = 1,000,000 → only ~20 steps (because log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20).

👉 Faster than O(√n) and O(n).

2. O(n log n)

Slightly slower than linear but still much better than quadratic.

Common in efficient sorting algorithms (Merge Sort, Quick Sort, Heap Sort).

Example: Sorting 1,000,000 numbers → ~20,000,000 operations (vs 1 trillion in O(n²)).

👉 Slower than O(n), faster than O(n²).
3. Relative Growth Comparison

Let’s rank them from best (fastest) to worst (slowest):

O(1) → Constant time (best)

O(log n) → Grows very slowly

O(√n) → Faster than linear but slower than log

O(n) → Linear growth

O(n log n) → Slightly worse than linear

O(n²) → Quadratic (bad for large n)

O(2ⁿ), O(n!) → Exponential/factorial (impossible for big n)

4. Analogy (to visualize 🚀)

Imagine you have to check 1,000,000 items:

O(1): Check directly → 1 step

O(log n): Halve each time → ~20 steps

O(√n): Walk √n → 1,000 steps

O(n): Check all → 1,000,000 steps

O(n log n): Sort them → 20,000,000 steps

O(n²): Compare everything with everything → 1,000,000,000,000 steps
